{
    "background_story": "Idris was a renowned epidemiologist who dedicated his life to studying noncommunicable diseases (NCDs). Growing up in a community ravaged by cancer, diabetes, and heart disease, he witnessed firsthand the devastating impact of NCDs on families and communities. Driven by a desire to make a difference, Idris pursued a career in public health, earning a Ph.D. in epidemiology. His groundbreaking research focused on identifying risk factors and developing effective prevention strategies for NCDs. Idris's work took him to various parts of the world, from urban slums to rural villages, where he collaborated with local health workers to implement innovative programs. His tireless efforts led to significant reductions in NCD-related mortality rates, earning him international recognition and accolades. Idris's legacy continues to inspire a new generation of health professionals to tackle the growing burden of noncommunicable diseases.",
